Sound in games that keep little queued no longer drops out: Wine sets the Mac's audio piece size to its own period.

From upstream Wine (wine-mirror/wine, LGPL-2.1-or-later): commits 56a4347 by Giovanni Mascellani and
07c7fcc, 8e09602 and da0b084 by Zhiyi Zhang, carried unchanged in this order. They came after CrossOver
26.3.0 and apply to it with no fuzz:

- 56a4347acac16b1445a55719e1fddd9c6f9581b5, Giovanni Mascellani: "winecoreaudio.drv: Avoid a division
  by zero." Carried because 07c7fcc's context sits next to it and does not apply without it.
- 07c7fcc86fe504415191095c42146362e2d3d93f, Zhiyi Zhang: "winecoreaudio: Set the requested period
  frame size when creating audio streams." The fix: the stream's period is clamped to the device's
  allowed range and set as the device's buffer frame size (kAudioDevicePropertyBufferFrameSize).
- 8e09602ca6e41d21fc6ee07b3a48ddeb6350a644, Zhiyi Zhang: "winecoreaudio: Add a helper to get device
  sample rate."
- da0b08472b4b46b202618056ad7c784b0f51acc8, Zhiyi Zhang: "winecoreaudio: Return the actual Core Audio
  device period frame size." The device period Wine reports is the Mac's piece size (512 frames at
  48 kHz is 10.67 ms), not a fixed 10 ms; its test in dlls/mmdevapi/tests/render.c accepts 106667.

Why mavrik needs it: Wine never set the Mac's piece size, so the Mac pulled 512 frames at a time while a
game's sound library (SDL2's WASAPI backend, as in Rogue Legacy) added 480 frames per 10 ms tick and only
once 480 or fewer were queued. The queue often ran dry and Wine filled the gap with silence, so the sound
was choppy. Upstream's own message names the same fault ("crackling sound").

Upstream status: in Wine master since 2026-08-17. Remove this patch when the pinned CrossOver sources
carry these commits.

